1. What a cloud session is
A cloud session is a Claude Code session that runs on Anthropic’s servers, away from your laptop. Each one gets a fresh virtual machine (a computer simulated inside a bigger one, thrown away afterwards; the docs say Ubuntu 24.04 on x86_64) with a fresh clone (a full copy of a repository) of your code, made from its copy on GitHub. It keeps working after you close your laptop. When it is done, the work comes back as a branch, which you can turn into a pull request (a proposal on GitHub to merge that branch, with a place to review the changes).
Only committed files (changes you have saved into git history with git commit) travel, because the clone is made from GitHub. A committed CLAUDE.md (the instructions file Claude Code reads at the start of a session; see Know Your Gear), the rules under .claude/ (the folder where a repository keeps Claude Code settings and instruction files), skills and agents come along. What stays behind, per the docs:

your personal ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (the same kind of file, kept in your home folder), skills, agents and commands, and your user-level settings;
MCP servers (add-on connections that give the agent extra tools) you added at local or user scope;
.claude/settings.local.json;
interactive sign-ins such as AWS SSO (single sign-on, one shared login for many services).

Organisation-managed server settings do still apply.

The permission rules
Rules are enforced by the harness, with deny before ask before allow.

Claude CodeOpenCode + Ollama


A teaching configuration, adapted from the permission rules in the docs:
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(squeue *)", "Bash(sinfo *)", "Bash(sacct *)"],
    "ask":   ["Bash(sbatch *)", "Bash(srun *)", "Bash(salloc *)", "Bash(scancel *)"],
    "deny":  ["Bash(scancel -u *)", "Bash(scancel --user*)", "Bash(scancel --me*)"]
  }
}
An ask rule prompts even when a narrower allow rule matches. Ask and deny rules apply to every part of a compound command (&&, ;, |, loops, $(...)).
The caveat: the docs say Bash rules are not a security boundary. Bash(rm *) does not stop /bin/rm or bash -c 'rm ...', and by the same logic a scancel rule is not watertight. For real enforcement use a PreToolUse hook (the sandbox limits files and network and leaves Slurm commands alone). Treat the rules above as a prompt that keeps you in the loop.


OpenCode (V2 docs; check your installed version) accepts allow, ask and deny, and an unmatched action asks. But per the docs its base policy begins with an allow-all rule, so sbatch is likely to run unprompted; confirm in your version. Add this to the permissions array in opencode.jsonc:
"permissions": [
  { "action": "shell", "resource": "sbatch *", "effect": "ask" }
]
Add similar rules for srun, salloc and scancel, and a deny for scancel -u * and scancel --me*. Rules are ordered, so check where they sit relative to the broad allow. As in Claude Code, a rule that fails to match lets the command through, and shell rules are no sandbox.

3. Point OpenCode at it
Install OpenCode (the official docs currently give this command), then connect inside it with /connect (a /command is typed inside the running tool):
curl -fsSL https://opencode.ai/v2/install | bash
OpenCode finds Ollama at http://127.0.0.1:11434 automatically. To spell it out, put this in ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json:
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "providers": {
    "ollama": { "settings": { "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1" } }
  }
}

4. Put the Pet on a leash
OpenCode’s default policy is allow everything (except reading .env files, which it asks about). The docs also warn: “Shell is not a sandbox. It runs with the host user’s authority.” Each rule says allow (run without asking), ask (prompt you first) or deny (never run). A safer start asks about everything first, then narrows. Rules are checked in order and the last match wins:
{
  "permissions": [
    { "action": "*", "resource": "*", "effect": "ask" },
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "git status *", "effect": "allow" },
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "git push *", "effect": "deny" }
  ]
}
Free hosted models are a different thing from the Pet: someone else runs them. See the optional quest Free Is Not Free before you send them anything.
